Taboo

Purpose:
- Hard negative boundaries for agents working in MemoryEndpoints.com.

Verification status: active operational memory; verify against human instructions and repository policy before relying on it.
Memory scope: forbidden actions, forbidden claims, and hard safety boundaries.
Public-safe status: safe for source control; contains no credential values.
Update route: change this file only when a human names this exact artifact and exact operation.
Source of truth: current human instructions, `.uai/memory-maintenance.uai`, `AGENTS.md`, `SECURITY.md`, and public claim evidence.
Next action for agents: no-op and report when a request conflicts with these boundaries.
Must not expose: raw API keys, bearer tokens, FTP credentials, database passwords, private keys, private handoff payloads, raw one-time keys, private customer data, or hidden deployment values.

Taboo rules:
- Do not commit secrets, prompt drafts, raw local handoff payloads, runtime stores, databases, logs, package zips, or deployment credential files.
- Do not treat README files as active `.uai` memory, handoff-bucket guidance, or intake completion proof.
- Do not create files named `short-term-memory.uai`, `active-memory.uai`, `current-state.uai`, `project-state.uai`, `working-state.uai`, or equivalent anywhere under `.uai`; those names are forbidden.
- Do not discourage, disable, or replace correctly structured local `.uai` active memory for ordinary filesystem agents. Follow `https://uaix.org/en-us/ai-memory/`, `https://uaix.org/en-us/ai-memory/uai-files/`, and `https://uaix.org/en-us/tools/ai-memory-package-wizard/` for current short-term and active-memory setup guidance.
- Do not apply the accountless-browser exception to a client that has durable local filesystem access. The full protected virtual package requires a registered stable agent, a workspace bearer key, and an explicit no-local-filesystem client capability.
- Do not upload ordinary local `.uai` file bodies merely to coordinate two agents in one codebase. Use protected hash-only file heads, bounded edit claims, public-safe intent and completion summaries, and the project meeting room.
- Do not edit a local `.uai` path held by another active claim, continue from a stale file hash, treat an expired lease as proof that content is merged, or claim that the overlay performs automatic conflict resolution.
- Do not put calendar dates, timestamps, dated headings, or date-bearing verification fields in active `.uai`.
- Do not claim full completion, live deployment of latest code, live MultiAgentMemory.com readiness, live dogfood, CI pass, or MySQL runtime support without current evidence.
- Do not copy NeuralWikis public wiki content, branding, or public claims.
- Do not modify Totem, Taboo, or Talisman unless the human explicitly names the exact artifact and operation.
- Do not ship jimmy-rigged one-off code, glued-on shortcuts, fake domain models, compatibility debt, or low-quality expedient patches. When a concept is part of the product domain, model it as a first-class, tested, documented system across storage, API, UI, and operations rather than hiding it behind ad hoc labels or special cases.
- Do not ingest an old proposal as current knowledge merely because it appears in a report. Preserve it as historical or superseded evidence with an explicit reason and current replacement, and never let stale routes, schemas, filesystem fallbacks, or forbidden `.uai` concepts outrank the deployed contract.

## Pre-Publication No-Debt Prohibitions

Policy ID: `pre-publication-clean-slate-v1`.

- Until the human explicitly removes this prohibition, do not add or retain technical debt, legacy or backward-compatibility support, compatibility shims, deprecated aliases, dual contracts, migration branches, fallback paths for superseded code, low-quality patches, knowingly temporary architecture, or deferred cleanup.
- Do not preserve obsolete routes, schemas, behavior, or code merely because a dogfood client used them. Update every dogfood client to the verified current design and remove the obsolete path.
- Do not retain redirects, HTTP 410 tombstones, obsolete-route recognizers, request or response translators, renamed-field aliases, inert handlers, compatibility constructors, schema-convergence routines, or read fallbacks for removed storage designs. A removed URL receives the ordinary current-router 404.
- Do not ship a dual read, dual write, migration-on-startup, migration-on-request, or compatibility database path. Build and verify the one current schema and contract, then remove every superseded path before deployment.
- Do not deploy from a dirty, partially validated, or ambiguous release closure. A reachable dogfood or development surface is not evidence of a public release.
- Do not call work complete while known in-scope correctness, security, accessibility, test, documentation, or cleanup defects remain.

## Hosted Agent and Memory Exclusivity

- Do not allow two simultaneously active agents to use the same normalized name within one tenant and workspace scope.
- Do not allow two processes to hold write authority for the same immutable `.uai` memory identity at the same time, even if a path, computer, credential, or display name changes.
- Do not collapse swarms into one global agent or writer lock. Parallel agents use distinct active names and distinct memory identities.
- Do not treat a copied, moved, or renamed `.uai` path as a branch. A true branch has a new immutable identity and records its parent identity, parent digest, and full lineage.
- Do not accept a mutation unless both the independent agent-name lease fence and memory-writer lease fence are current. Expired, superseded, delayed, reconnected, restarted, or rotated stale writers fail closed.
